The original hexadecimal byte strings for clientDataJSON, attestationObject,
authenticatorData, signature, challenge, and credential ID were converted to
unpadded base64url and packaged as WebAuthn response JSON. Names, relying
party, origin, verification policy and expected refusal annotations were added.
Sections 16.2 through 16.16 with supported credential algorithms are included;
16.9 uses ES512 and 16.12 uses Ed448, neither supported by std/cose. TPM,
Android Key, Apple and FIDO U2F statements are retained to exercise Ignore,
not claimed as verified attestation formats. The cross-origin examples are
expected refusals under Relic's policy. Section 16.6's 1023-byte credential
ID is valid; a separate software case checks refusal above that limit. W3C's
extension examples are API-shape examples, not complete ceremony signatures.

Chrome virtual-authenticator fixture

chrome.json was captured from HeadlessChrome/156.0.8078.4 (Playwright
chromium_headless_shell-1248, macOS arm64). A throwaway headless shell was
started with its own temporary user-data-dir and remote-debugging port. A
loopback HTTP server at http://localhost:49655 served a blank page. CDP
Target.createTarget, Target.attachToTarget, WebAuthn.enable, and
WebAuthn.addVirtualAuthenticator enabled a CTAP2 internal authenticator with
user verification and automatic presence. Runtime.evaluate ran
navigator.credentials.create with ES256, attestation none and required user
verification, then navigator.credentials.get with the returned credential and
a separate challenge. Both responses and the supplied challenges were
serialized as WebAuthn JSON. The shell and profile were removed afterwards.
No browser profile, real authenticator or real user credential was used.

